KG42.register("nodes-749cf0ae997b0b68d78c6bd6",{"id":"COMP-6b28b8426784699b","code":"SET002","label":"Nash-bargaining transfer payments after fixing the optimal schedule","definition":"After the P1 optimal dispatch is fixed, P2 maximizes ∏_{i∈M′}(δ_i^*−C_e(π_i)) with respect to {π_i, i∈M′}, where C_e(π_i)=Σ_{j∈M\\{i}}π_{i,j} and δ_i^*=C_i^{Non}−CO_i(q_i^*,x_n^*,r_{c,i}^*,r_{d,i}^*); payments satisfy π_{i,j}+π_{j,i}=0 and operating cost plus net payment does not exceed C_i^{Non}, according to which cooperative gains are distributed.","layer":"L8","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"settlement.payment","concept_ids":["settlement.payment"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: bilateral monetary transfers π_{i,j} and participant net payments C_e(π_i)","Core operation: maximize the product of cooperative gains after deducting net payments","Constraint relation: bilateral payment balance and individual rationality relative to the no-transaction benchmark","Output: the bargaining payment vector and participants' net benefits"]},"exclusions":["P1 first determines the dispatch and cost reduction; P2 targets only the M′ corresponding to nonzero transaction vectors","π_{i,j}>0 indicates that i pays j; π_{i,j}\u003c0 indicates that i receives payment from j","Payments are determined through bargaining and need not be linearly related to the exchanged electricity quantity","Individual rationality retains CO_i(q_i,x_n,r_{c,i},r_{d,i})+C_e(π_i)≤C_i^{Non}; P2 uses the fixed P1 optimal dispatch","The virtual clearing house receives payment reports; the distributed implementation uses consistency updates of π̂, γ, and ρ2"],"members":[{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Microgrids submit the requested quantities in their purchasing schedules to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. Condition: For reported schedule entries with e_{i,j}^t > 0, representing purchases by microgrid i from microgrid j. Microgrids submit the offered quantities in their selling schedules to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. Condition: For reported schedule entries with e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0, representing sales by microgrid i to microgrid j. Microgrids report their energy-trading payments to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Payment reporting by participating interconnected microgrids during the decentralized solution of P2. The virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to the prescribed update rules and broadcasts them to all participating microgrids. Scope: Virtual clearing house coordination at the beginning of each daily operation of the interconnected-microgrid market. Condition: When solving P1 and P2, respectively.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. | Payment reporting by participating interconnected microgrids during the decentralized solution of P2. | Virtual clearing house coordination at the beginning of each daily operation of the interconnected-microgrid market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E37"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"fa9ac411ec25165f59e98ff224e8dbe855aa3728d070df0b33b46e6435e88ac5","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"Social operating cost minimization P1 is solved before the trading microgrids determine their payments through P2. Scope: The two-stage decomposition of NBP for interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids and P2 covers the trading set M′. P2 uses each trading microgrid's operating-cost reduction from the optimal P1 solution to maximize the product of gains remaining after trading payments. Scope: Determination of P2 payments for microgrids in M′ using the optimal P1 solution. Participation in P2 payment bargaining is conditional on having a nonzero inter-microgrid trading allocation from P1. Scope: Eligibility of interconnected microgrids for P2 payment bargaining following P1 allocation. Condition: The microgrid has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i in the P1 solution, placing it in M′. Identifying the group of trading microgrids after excluding non-participants precedes allocating that group's benefits through payment bargaining. Scope: Identification of the active trading group M′ and allocation of its members' benefits through P2. The virtual clearing house provides the clearing service for inter-microgrid payments. Scope: The virtual clearing house's payment-clearing service for participating interconnected microgrids. Bilateral payment entries must balance: π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0. Scope: Bilateral payment entries for every distinct pair of interconnected microgrids. Operating cost plus net trading payment must not exceed the microgrid's minimized cost without inter-microgrid trading. Scope: The individual-rationality restriction on each microgrid's net trading payment. Condition: The microgrid participates in inter-microgrid energy trading. Payments are determined through bargaining and need not be linear in the exchanged energy. Scope: Payments negotiated between interconnected microgrids.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Bilateral payment entries for every distinct pair of interconnected microgrids. | Determination of P2 payments for microgrids in M′ using the optimal P1 solution. | Eligibility of interconnected microgrids for P2 payment bargaining following P1 allocation. | Identification of the active trading group M′ and allocation of its members' benefits through P2. | Payments negotiated between interconnected microgrids. | The individual-rationality restriction on each microgrid's net trading payment. | The two-stage decomposition of NBP for interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids and P2 covers the trading set M′. | The virtual clearing house's payment-clearing service for participating interconnected microgrids.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e05","v141-b-g01-e22","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E37"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C004","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C005","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C006","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C007","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C008","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C010"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"3ae08d924e0c3ef7eed14eebf713467170c3def7b5517b0d8f2c8dc443a658d2","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["07-nash_bargaining"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E33","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.B Solving P2 (Payment Bargaining)","anchor_text":"ρ2 2 + ˆπj,i −πj,i(k + 1) + γj,i(k) ˆπj,i 2 tra subject to ˆπi,j + ˆπj,i = 0, tri variables: ˆπi,j, ","equation_id":"(25)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"and obtain the optimal closed-form solution for updating π̂_{i,j}:\nπ̂_{i,j}(k+1) = −π̂_{j,i}(k+1)\n= [ ρ2 ( π_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{j,i}(k+1) ) − ( γ_{i,j}(k) − γ_{j,i}(k) ) ] / (2ρ2).   (25)","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"and obtain the optimal closed-form solution for updating π̂_{i,j}:\nπ̂_{i,j}(k+1) = −π̂_{j,i}(k+1)\n= [ ρ2 ( π_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{j,i}(k+1) ) − ( γ_{i,j}(k) − γ_{j,i}(k) ) ] / (2ρ2).   (25)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.B Solving P2 (Payment Bargaining)","anchor_text":"ρ2 2 + ˆπj,i −πj,i(k + 1) + γj,i(k) ˆπj,i 2 tra subject to ˆπi,j + ˆπj,i = 0, tri variables: ˆπi,j, ","equation_id":"(26)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Based on π_{i,j}(k+1) and π̂_{i,j}(k+1), we update the dual variables as follows:\nγ_{i,j}(k+1) = γ_{i,j}(k) + ρ2 ( π̂_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{i,j}(k+1) ).   (26)","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Based on π_{i,j}(k+1) and π̂_{i,j}(k+1), we update the dual variables as follows:\nγ_{i,j}(k+1) = γ_{i,j}(k) + ρ2 ( π̂_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{i,j}(k+1) ).   (26)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"scheduling {gi , qi , xn, rc,i, rd,i, ei } for each microgrid i ∈M. us If a microgrid i has a nonzer","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P2: Payment bargaining problem:\nmax ∏_{i∈M′} ( δ_i^* − Ce(π_i) )\nsubject to (16) and (18),\nvariables: { π_i, i ∈ M′ },\nwhere δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*) denotes the operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"P2: Payment bargaining problem:\nmax ∏_{i∈M′} ( δ_i^* − Ce(π_i) )\nsubject to (16) and (18),\nvariables: { π_i, i ∈ M′ },\nwhere δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*) denotes the operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem:\nmin ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i})\nsubject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),\nvariables: { g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i, i ∈ M }.\nSolving P1 determines the optimal energy trading & scheduling {g_i^*, q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*, e_i^*} for each microgrid i ∈ M. If a microgrid i has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i, then it belongs to set M′, and will participate in the payment bargaining in Problem P2. For the non-trading microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they do not participate in the payment bargaining and their operating costs are the same as the disagreement points C_j^{Non}.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem:\nmin ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i})\nsubject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),\nvariables: { g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i, i ∈ M }.\nSolving P1 determines the optimal energy trading & scheduling {g_i^*, q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*, e_i^*} for each microgrid i ∈ M. If a microgrid i has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i, then it belongs to set M′, and will participate in the payment bargaining in Problem P2. For the non-trading microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they do not participate in the payment bargaining and their operating costs are the same as the disagreement points C_j^{Non}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":"2653","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"WANG AND HUANG: INCENTIVIZING ENERGY TRADING FOR INTERCONNECTED M microgrids do not contribute to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"microgrids do not contribute to the social cost minimization and won't benefit from energy trading. The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization. Secondly, after identifying those microgrids who do not want to participate in the energy trading, we then focus on the rest of the microgrids who trade energy. We decide a fair allocation of benefits to each of participated microgrids through the payment bargaining problem P2.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"microgrids do not contribute to the social cost minimization and won't benefit from energy trading. The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization. Secondly, after identifying those microgrids who do not want to participate in the energy trading, we then focus on the rest of the microgrids who trade energy. We decide a fair allocation of benefits to each of participated microgrids through the payment bargaining problem P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e22","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M, (15) π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M. (16)","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M, (15) π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M. (16)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0,   ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M.   (16)   The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: ...","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0,   ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M.   (16)   The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: ..."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint: CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + C_e(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non}, ∀i ∈ M. (18) Here the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint: CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + C_e(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non}, ∀i ∈ M. (18) Here the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint:\nCO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + Ce(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non},   ∀i ∈ M.   (18)\nHere the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint:\nCO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + Ce(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non},   ∀i ∈ M.   (18)\nHere the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"o- j∈M\\i n∈Ni ed ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (17) nd where j∈M\\i eti,j is the net energy traded between microgrid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MG_i, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e., C_e(π_i) = Σ_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}. The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operating cost C_i^O(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) and trading payment C_e(π_i).","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MG_i, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e., C_e(π_i) = Σ_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}. The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operating cost C_i^O(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) and trading payment C_e(π_i)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"o- j∈M\\i n∈Ni ed ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (17) nd where j∈M\\i eti,j is the net energy traded between microgrid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MGi, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e.,\nCe(π_i) = ∑_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}.","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MGi, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e.,\nCe(π_i) = ∑_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["nash_bargaining_transfer_payment"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L8-797628394f","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L8","ontological_kind":"nash_bargaining_transfer_payment"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"07-nash_bargaining","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Microgrids submit the requested quantities in their purchasing schedules to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. Condition: For reported schedule entries with e_{i,j}^t > 0, representing purchases by microgrid i from microgrid j. Microgrids submit the offered quantities in their selling schedules to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. Condition: For reported schedule entries with e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0, representing sales by microgrid i to microgrid j. Microgrids report their energy-trading payments to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Payment reporting by participating interconnected microgrids during the decentralized solution of P2. The virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to the prescribed update rules and broadcasts them to all participating microgrids. Scope: Virtual clearing house coordination at the beginning of each daily operation of the interconnected-microgrid market. Condition: When solving P1 and P2, respectively.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Energy-quantity reporting during the decentralized P1 iterations among interconnected microgrids. | Payment reporting by participating interconnected microgrids during the decentralized solution of P2. | Virtual clearing house coordination at the beginning of each daily operation of the interconnected-microgrid market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E37"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"fa9ac411ec25165f59e98ff224e8dbe855aa3728d070df0b33b46e6435e88ac5"},{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"Social operating cost minimization P1 is solved before the trading microgrids determine their payments through P2. Scope: The two-stage decomposition of NBP for interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids and P2 covers the trading set M′. P2 uses each trading microgrid's operating-cost reduction from the optimal P1 solution to maximize the product of gains remaining after trading payments. Scope: Determination of P2 payments for microgrids in M′ using the optimal P1 solution. Participation in P2 payment bargaining is conditional on having a nonzero inter-microgrid trading allocation from P1. Scope: Eligibility of interconnected microgrids for P2 payment bargaining following P1 allocation. Condition: The microgrid has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i in the P1 solution, placing it in M′. Identifying the group of trading microgrids after excluding non-participants precedes allocating that group's benefits through payment bargaining. Scope: Identification of the active trading group M′ and allocation of its members' benefits through P2. The virtual clearing house provides the clearing service for inter-microgrid payments. Scope: The virtual clearing house's payment-clearing service for participating interconnected microgrids. Bilateral payment entries must balance: π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0. Scope: Bilateral payment entries for every distinct pair of interconnected microgrids. Operating cost plus net trading payment must not exceed the microgrid's minimized cost without inter-microgrid trading. Scope: The individual-rationality restriction on each microgrid's net trading payment. Condition: The microgrid participates in inter-microgrid energy trading. Payments are determined through bargaining and need not be linear in the exchanged energy. Scope: Payments negotiated between interconnected microgrids.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Bilateral payment entries for every distinct pair of interconnected microgrids. | Determination of P2 payments for microgrids in M′ using the optimal P1 solution. | Eligibility of interconnected microgrids for P2 payment bargaining following P1 allocation. | Identification of the active trading group M′ and allocation of its members' benefits through P2. | Payments negotiated between interconnected microgrids. | The individual-rationality restriction on each microgrid's net trading payment. | The two-stage decomposition of NBP for interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids and P2 covers the trading set M′. | The virtual clearing house's payment-clearing service for participating interconnected microgrids.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e05","v141-b-g01-e22","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E37"]},"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C004","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C005","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C006","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C007","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C008","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C010"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"3ae08d924e0c3ef7eed14eebf713467170c3def7b5517b0d8f2c8dc443a658d2"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Microgrids report their energy-trading payments to the virtual clearing house. Scope: Payment reporting by participating interconnected microgrids during the decentralized solution of P2.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment","coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E37"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"For every ordered pair of distinct microgrids (i,j), the signed payment π_{i,j} is positive if and only if microgrid i makes a payment to microgrid j, and is negative if and only if microgrid i receives a payment from microgrid j.","expression":"\\pi_{i,j} > 0 \\Longleftrightarrow \\text{payment from } i \\text{ to } j; \\quad \\pi_{i,j} \u003c 0 \\Longleftrightarrow \\text{payment from } j \\text{ to } i","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment","coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["pi_ij"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"At iteration k+1, the auxiliary payment variable \\hat{\\pi}_{i,j} is updated by the closed-form rule (25), which is antisymmetric in the trading pair and uses the current local payments and dual variables.","expression":"\\hat{\\pi}_{i,j}(k+1) = -\\hat{\\pi}_{j,i}(k+1) = \\frac{\\rho_2 ( \\pi_{i,j}(k+1) - \\pi_{j,i}(k+1) ) - ( \\gamma_{i,j}(k) - \\gamma_{j,i}(k) )}{2 \\rho_2}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["pi_hat_ij","pi_ij","gamma_ij"],"parameter_refs":["rho2"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E33","v141-b-g02-E37"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E33","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.B Solving P2 (Payment Bargaining)","anchor_text":"ρ2 2 + ˆπj,i −πj,i(k + 1) + γj,i(k) ˆπj,i 2 tra subject to ˆπi,j + ˆπj,i = 0, tri variables: ˆπi,j, ","equation_id":"(25)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"and obtain the optimal closed-form solution for updating π̂_{i,j}:\nπ̂_{i,j}(k+1) = −π̂_{j,i}(k+1)\n= [ ρ2 ( π_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{j,i}(k+1) ) − ( γ_{i,j}(k) − γ_{j,i}(k) ) ] / (2ρ2).   (25)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C010","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"At iteration k+1, the dual variable \\gamma_{i,j} associated with the P2 payment consistency constraint is updated by the residual between the auxiliary and actual payments.","expression":"\\gamma_{i,j}(k+1) = \\gamma_{i,j}(k) + \\rho_2 ( \\hat{\\pi}_{i,j}(k+1) - \\pi_{i,j}(k+1) )","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["gamma_ij","pi_hat_ij","pi_ij"],"parameter_refs":["rho2"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E34","v141-b-g02-E37"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.B Solving P2 (Payment Bargaining)","anchor_text":"ρ2 2 + ˆπj,i −πj,i(k + 1) + γj,i(k) ˆπj,i 2 tra subject to ˆπi,j + ˆπj,i = 0, tri variables: ˆπi,j, ","equation_id":"(26)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Based on π_{i,j}(k+1) and π̂_{i,j}(k+1), we update the dual variables as follows:\nγ_{i,j}(k+1) = γ_{i,j}(k) + ρ2 ( π̂_{i,j}(k+1) − π_{i,j}(k+1) ).   (26)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Social operating cost minimization P1 is solved before the trading microgrids determine their payments through P2. Scope: The two-stage decomposition of NBP for interconnected microgrids; P1 covers all microgrids and P2 covers the trading set M′.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"P2 uses each trading microgrid's operating-cost reduction from the optimal P1 solution to maximize the product of gains remaining after trading payments. Scope: Determination of P2 payments for microgrids in M′ using the optimal P1 solution.","expression":"P2: max_{π_i, i∈M′} ∏_{i∈M′} ( δ_i^* − C_e(π_i) ), subject to (16) and (18), with δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-pi-i","v141-c-R009-x-pi-ij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-th-delta"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"scheduling {gi , qi , xn, rc,i, rd,i, ei } for each microgrid i ∈M. us If a microgrid i has a nonzer","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P2: Payment bargaining problem:\nmax ∏_{i∈M′} ( δ_i^* − Ce(π_i) )\nsubject to (16) and (18),\nvariables: { π_i, i ∈ M′ },\nwhere δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*) denotes the operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Participation in P2 payment bargaining is conditional on having a nonzero inter-microgrid trading allocation from P1. Scope: Eligibility of interconnected microgrids for P2 payment bargaining following P1 allocation. Condition: The microgrid has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i in the P1 solution, placing it in M′.","expression":"e_i ≠ 0 ⇒ i ∈ M′ (i participates in P2); M′ = { i ∈ M : i trades energy with other microgrids }, so i ∈ M′ ⇒ e_i ≠ 0; with e_i = { e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i }; for j ∈ M\\M′, j does not participate in P2 and its operating cost equals its disagreement point C_j^{Non}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-th-ei"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E18","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P1: Social operating cost minimization problem:\nmin ∑_{i∈M} CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i})\nsubject to (1)–(3), (5), (6), (8)–(11), (14), (15), and (17),\nvariables: { g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i, i ∈ M }.\nSolving P1 determines the optimal energy trading & scheduling {g_i^*, q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*, e_i^*} for each microgrid i ∈ M. If a microgrid i has a nonzero energy trading vector e_i, then it belongs to set M′, and will participate in the payment bargaining in Problem P2. For the non-trading microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they do not participate in the payment bargaining and their operating costs are the same as the disagreement points C_j^{Non}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Identifying the group of trading microgrids after excluding non-participants precedes allocating that group's benefits through payment bargaining. Scope: Identification of the active trading group M′ and allocation of its members' benefits through P2.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"BP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy d- trading & scheduling and trading p","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The energy-trading bargaining problem NBP can be decomposed into two sequential subproblems on energy trading & scheduling and trading payment, denoted as P1 and P2, respectively. In the first step, we solve a social operating cost minimization problem P1 for the interconnected-microgrids system, which yields the optimal energy scheduling and energy trading for all microgrids. Those microgrids that trade energy with other microgrids form the set M′. In the second step, microgrids in set M′ bargain with each other to determine the energy-trading payments in P2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":"2653","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"WANG AND HUANG: INCENTIVIZING ENERGY TRADING FOR INTERCONNECTED M microgrids do not contribute to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"microgrids do not contribute to the social cost minimization and won't benefit from energy trading. The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization. Secondly, after identifying those microgrids who do not want to participate in the energy trading, we then focus on the rest of the microgrids who trade energy. We decide a fair allocation of benefits to each of participated microgrids through the payment bargaining problem P2."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The virtual clearing house provides the clearing service for inter-microgrid payments. Scope: The virtual clearing house's payment-clearing service for participating interconnected microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E37"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E37","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":"2654","section":"V.C Algorithm Design and Implementation","anchor_text":"To implement the solution method for solving the energy trading and payment problems P1 and P2, we d","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We introduce the concept of virtual clearing house for solving the higher level problem. The virtual clearing house is a communication and computing module, which provides clearing service of energy trading and payment for all the microgrids. At the beginning of each daily operation, the virtual clearing house updates the auxiliary and dual variables according to (21), (22) and (25), (26), and broadcasts to all participating microgrids when solving P1 and P2, respectively. All the participating microgrids concurrently solve P1-MGi and P2-MGi and report their energy trading schedules and payments to the virtual clearing house."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Bilateral payment entries must balance: π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0. Scope: Bilateral payment entries for every distinct pair of interconnected microgrids.","expression":"π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M. (16)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-pi-ij"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e22","v141-b-g02-E10"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e22","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: e_{i,j}^t + e_{j,i}^t = 0, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M, (15) π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0, ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M. (16)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"NECTED MICROGRIDS 2651 ue The energy trading and payment among microgrids should di- satisfy the mar","equation_id":"(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"π_{i,j} + π_{j,i} = 0,   ∀j ∈ M\\i, ∀i ∈ M.   (16)   The energy trading and payment among microgrids should satisfy the market clearing constraints: ..."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Operating cost plus net trading payment must not exceed the microgrid's minimized cost without inter-microgrid trading. Scope: The individual-rationality restriction on each microgrid's net trading payment. Condition: The microgrid participates in inter-microgrid energy trading.","expression":"CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + C_e(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non}, ∀i ∈ M. (18)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-pi-i"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-th-non"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e05","v141-b-g02-E13"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint: CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + C_e(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non}, ∀i ∈ M. (18) Here the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint:\nCO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + Ce(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non},   ∀i ∈ M.   (18)\nHere the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Payments are determined through bargaining and need not be linear in the exchanged energy. Scope: Payments negotiated between interconnected microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider that each microgrid i ∈ M bargains with all the other interconnected microgrids to determine the amount of energy trading e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i} and the associated payment π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}. Here e_{i,j}^t denotes the amount of energy that microgrid i exchanges with microgrid j in time slot t, and π_{i,j} denotes the associated payment for energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j. If microgrid i purchases energy from microgrid j in time slot t, then e_{i,j}^t > 0; otherwise, microgrid i sells energy to microgrid j and e_{i,j}^t \u003c 0. Similarly, if microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, then π_{i,j} > 0; otherwise microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j and π_{i,j} \u003c 0. 4Note that the payments π_i are determined through bargaining between microgrids, and are not necessary linear in terms of the energy exchange e_i."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The extra cost of an interconnected microgrid is its total trading payment to all other microgrids: C_e(π_i) = Σ_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}. (Payment aggregation entering the individual-rationality constraint (18) and the P2 objective.)","expression":"C_e(π_i) = Σ_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}, ∀i ∈ M.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-pi-i","v141-c-R009-x-pi-ij"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-E12"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"o- j∈M\\i n∈Ni ed ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (17) nd where j∈M\\i eti,j is the net energy traded between microgrid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MG_i, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e., C_e(π_i) = Σ_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}. The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operating cost C_i^O(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) and trading payment C_e(π_i)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"o- j∈M\\i n∈Ni ed ∀t ∈T , ∀i ∈M, (17) nd where j∈M\\i eti,j is the net energy traded between microgrid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Compared with the cost function of microgrid i in Problem P-MGi, the interconnected microgrid has an extra cost that is the payment to other microgrids, i.e.,\nCe(π_i) = ∑_{j∈M\\i} π_{i,j}."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C010","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Definition of the operating-cost reduction used in the P2 objective: δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*) denotes the operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1.","expression":"δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*), i ∈ M′.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-th-non","v141-c-R009-th-delta"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"],"ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"scheduling {gi , qi , xn, rc,i, rd,i, ei } for each microgrid i ∈M. us If a microgrid i has a nonzer","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"P2: Payment bargaining problem:\nmax ∏_{i∈M′} ( δ_i^* − Ce(π_i) )\nsubject to (16) and (18),\nvariables: { π_i, i ∈ M′ },\nwhere δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*) denotes the operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"pi_ij","symbol":"\\pi_{i,j}","meaning":"Payment associated with energy trading between microgrid i and microgrid j; positive means microgrid i makes payment to microgrid j, negative means microgrid i receives payment from microgrid j.","kind":"operational","domain":"ℝ","unit":null,"indices":["i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::x::pi_ij"},{"variable_id":"pi_hat_ij","symbol":"\\hat{\\pi}_{i,j}","meaning":"Auxiliary payment variable introduced for ADMM decomposition of P2, enforcing consistency with the actual payment \\pi_{i,j} and antisymmetry between trading partners.","kind":"operational","domain":"ℝ","unit":null,"indices":["i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E30","v141-b-g02-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::x::pi_hat_ij"},{"variable_id":"gamma_ij","symbol":"\\gamma_{i,j}","meaning":"Dual variable associated with the payment consistency constraint \\hat{\\pi}_{i,j} = \\pi_{i,j} in the ADMM solution of P2.","kind":"operational","domain":"ℝ","unit":null,"indices":["i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E34"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::x::gamma_ij"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-pi-ij","symbol":"π_{i,j}","meaning":"Payment made by microgrid i to microgrid j for inter-microgrid energy trading; π_{i,j} > 0 means i pays j, π_{i,j} \u003c 0 means i receives payment from j. Bargained decision variable of the P2 payment-bargaining problem.","kind":"operational","domain":"signed real (π_{i,j} > 0 if i pays j; π_{i,j} \u003c 0 if i receives from j)","unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ M","j ∈ M\\i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-pi-ij"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-pi-i","symbol":"π_i = {π_{i,j}, ∀j ∈ M\\i}","meaning":"Payment vector of microgrid i over all other interconnected microgrids; the decision-variable set of the P2 payment-bargaining problem is {π_i, i ∈ M′}.","kind":"operational","domain":"vector over j ∈ M\\i of signed real payments","unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ M′","j ∈ M\\i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-pi-i"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"rho2","symbol":"\\rho_2","meaning":"Penalty parameter for the ADMM solution of P2; positive algorithmic input.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E33"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::theta::rho2"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-th-non","symbol":"C_i^{Non}","meaning":"Minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids (the optimal value of P-MGi); the disagreement point of the individual-rationality constraint (18) and the benchmark of δ_i^*.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C_i^{Non}"},"unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e05","v141-b-g02-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-th-non"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-th-delta","symbol":"δ_i^*","meaning":"Operating cost reduction of microgrid i based on the optimal solution of Problem P1: δ_i^* ≜ C_i^{Non} − CO_i(q_i^*, x_n^*, r_{c,i}^*, r_{d,i}^*).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"δ_i^*"},"unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ M′"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-th-delta"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-th-ei","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"Inter-microgrid energy trading vector of microgrid i, e_i = {e_{i,j}^t, ∀t ∈ T, ∀j ∈ M\\i}; used here at its P1-optimal value. A nonzero e_i places i in the trading set M′. Scope note: e_i is a decision variable of the preceding P1/clearing problem and a fixed input to this settlement rule.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"e_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ M","j ∈ M\\i","t ∈ T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-th-ei"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"nash_bargaining_transfer_payment","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"After the P1 optimal dispatch is fixed, P2 maximizes ∏_{i∈M′}(δ_i^*−C_e(π_i)) with respect to {π_i, i∈M′}, where C_e(π_i)=Σ_{j∈M\\{i}}π_{i,j} and δ_i^*=C_i^{Non}−CO_i(q_i^*,x_n^*,r_{c,i}^*,r_{d,i}^*); payments satisfy π_{i,j}+π_{j,i}=0 and operating cost plus net payment does not exceed C_i^{Non}, according to which cooperative gains are distributed.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: bilateral monetary transfers π_{i,j} and participant net payments C_e(π_i)","Core operation: maximize the product of cooperative gains after deducting net payments","Constraint relation: bilateral payment balance and individual rationality relative to the no-transaction benchmark","Output: the bargaining payment vector and participants' net benefits"],"variation_fields":["P1 first determines the dispatch and cost reduction; P2 targets only the M′ corresponding to nonzero transaction vectors","π_{i,j}>0 indicates that i pays j; π_{i,j}\u003c0 indicates that i receives payment from j","Payments are determined through bargaining and need not be linearly related to the exchanged electricity quantity","Individual rationality retains CO_i(q_i,x_n,r_{c,i},r_{d,i})+C_e(π_i)≤C_i^{Non}; P2 uses the fixed P1 optimal dispatch","The virtual clearing house receives payment reports; the distributed implementation uses consistency updates of π̂, γ, and ρ2"],"constraint_refs":["07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C002","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C006","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C007","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C008","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"paper_id":"07-nash_bargaining","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/136"});
